            <ArticleTitle>[Calcification of coronary arteries and dyslipidemia in women with rheumatoid arthritis].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>55-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">to identify features of lipid profile in women with rheumatoid arthritis (RA) depending on calcification of coronary arteries (CA).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We examined 40 women with RA (mean age 48.6 ± 1.4 years, average duration of RA 7.77 ± 1 years) and 22 women of control group. Coronary artery calcification was detected by multislice computed tomography and expressed as Agatston score.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Frequency and degree of CA calcification in patients with RA was comparable with those in control group (=0.163 and =0.734, respectively). Calcification of CA in RA patients was accompanied by elevated levels of total and low density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol, non-high density lipoprotein cholesterol, LDL, and atherogeneity index. In all patients with CA calcification LDL cholesterol level exceeded 2.5 mmol/l.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
